    39	## What the issuer does (revision 3, after `CODEX-CONFIRM.md` ILC-01 to ILC-05; steps 3-6 and 10 revision 4, after `CODEX-CONFIRM-2.md` ILC2-01 to ILC2-03)
    40	
    41	The second revision let any worker latch or expire, and relied on compare-and-set plus re-observation. Codex found
    42	interleavings in which a stale or partial fetch expires an attempt that another, stalled worker had already observed
    43	on time (ILC-01). Compare-and-set makes the writes exclusive. It does not make the earlier observation win. Revision 3
    44	removes the concurrency instead of arbitrating it.
    45	
    46	1. **One writer.** Every lifecycle transition (start an attempt, latch, discard, expire, refuse, complete) is made
    47	   only by the poller, which runs with reserved concurrency 1 (`service/issuer/infra/main.tf:354`). The API is
    48	   read-only for the lifecycle: `api.status` no longer calls `settle` (`api.py:194`). Every transition is a write
    49	   guarded on the expected state *and* attempt id, so a write meant for an ended attempt fails (ILC-05).
    50	2. **Attempt isolation.** As before: each attempt's declared time, frames, signature jobs, proofs and evidence live
    51	   under its id. A late response names its attempt and can never fill another.
    52	3. **Latch (revision 4).** On each poll, for each open attempt, the poller fetches and upgrades the attempt's
    53	   evidence and evaluates the predicate. If it holds, the poller writes the latch with `observed_at` and the
    54	   qualifying presentation itself, not only its digests. A latch may be written at any time while the attempt is
    55	   open, however late the observation. Timeliness is a chain fact (the registered model's `burialAtWall`,
    56	   `formal/tla/P5cP5P6_BridgeSlack_Latch.tla:49, :68-71`), not the moment of the issuer's write.
    57	4. **Expiry only on a conclusive negative (revision 4, after `CODEX-CONFIRM-2.md` ILC2-01 to ILC2-03).** Absence of
    58	   evidence is never evidence of absence. An unlatched attempt expires only when, after B, the poller's own evidence
    59	   for it is **complete** and fails the predicate. Complete means three things:
    60	   - every calendar the attempt was stamped to has returned a Bitcoin attestation;
    61	   - for every attestation, the full pinned, linked `h…h+5` segment has been obtained (profile §7.1,
    62	     `270ca49:docs/V1-PROFILE.md:879-891`);
    63	   - so the lowest qualifying height, its T and its confirmation are fixed and fail a conjunct.
    64	
    65	   Stale, pending, partial or unavailable evidence never expires an attempt. No lease timing is relied on: whatever
    66	   a killed invocation saw, a later invocation will see at least the same complete evidence. Calendars do not
    67	   retract, the chain is public, and shallower reorganisations are absorbed by k-deep burial.
    68	5. **Evidence that stays unobtainable.** If an attempt's evidence cannot be completed, it stays open. After an
    69	   operating period past B (runbook), an *unlatched* attempt whose evidence is still incomplete may be ended with
    70	   `EVIDENCE_UNAVAILABLE`. This rests on A2.1's permission, "an attempt whose lag exceeds S may be refused"
    71	   (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:135-138`), and on A2.3's termination obligation (`:288-289`). A latched attempt
    72	   is never ended for unavailability.
    73	
    74	   **Stated residual:** an on-time observation by an invocation killed before its write, followed by permanent loss
    75	   of the evidence for the whole operating period. This is the one case where a refused attempt might have been
    76	   observed eligible by B. It is named in the model as a witness (step 10), not hidden here.
    77	6. **Permanent failure before B.** The same rule as step 4: discard before B only on complete evidence that fails
    78	   a conjunct.
    79	7. **After the latch.** The preserved qualifying presentation is used for assembly; it is never swapped for a
    80	   different, worse one. A transient failure (store, explorer, calendar, DNS snapshot) is retried. The attempt is
    81	   discarded only for a failure the frozen attempt cannot cure even with recovered evidence: T of the preserved
    82	   presentation > not-after (A9 `:422`), or a verifier reason that depends only on the attempt's committed bytes.
    83	   Each discard records its reason (ILC-04).
    84	8. **Next attempt and refusal.** Ending attempt i and allocating attempt i+1 happen in one guarded write. So a crash
    85	   cannot leave the request between attempts, and a retry cannot run twice. If i = N, or a new attempt would need
    86	   signing after the stop, the same write ends the request REFUSED with `ATTEMPTS_EXHAUSTED`, `SIGNING_CLOSED` or
    87	   `EVIDENCE_UNAVAILABLE`. A closed signing window never ends a latched or already signed and stamped attempt.
    88	9. **Public view and tolerance.** `fail_after` is withdrawn. The status shows `state`, `attempt` i of N, and for an
    89	   open, unlatched attempt its B, with a note that the twelve days are nominal. The 15-minute visibility tolerance
    90	   applies as stated; it never moves B.
    91	10. **Mechanized check before code** (revision 4 adds the cases from ILC2: observation-to-send delay, a commit
    92	    delayed after the send, a crash before persistence, timely burial with no observation, partial header recovery,
    93	    and duplicate transition retries; the property is the registered one, on `burialAtWall`, not on the durable
    94	    latch). The protocol in steps 1–8 (one writer, a killed invocation's delayed write,
